# PHASE 5 — PREMIUM PRODUCT-TO-CART FLIGHT ANIMATION

Implement a premium product image flight animation when the user clicks Add to Cart.

The effect should feel polished, smooth, natural, and professional.

## 5.1 Expected animation

When a user clicks Add to Cart on a product card:

1. Capture the actual product image and its visible screen position.
2. Identify the destination Navbar Cart Icon position.
3. Create a temporary visual representation of the product image.
4. Animate the image from the product card toward the Navbar Cart Icon.
5. Use a smooth, controlled, natural motion.
6. Gradually reduce the image size.
7. Add a subtle rotation or scale effect if visually appropriate.
8. End the animation at the cart icon.
9. Remove the temporary animation element.
10. Update the cart icon with a subtle confirmation animation.

### Visual quality

The animation must be:

- Premium.
- Smooth.
- Elegant.
- Responsive.
- Lightweight.
- Consistent with the NOVARA design system.

Avoid:

- Extremely fast movement.
- Abrupt disappearance.
- Excessive bouncing.
- Unnecessary particle effects.
- Distracting animations.
- Layout shifts.
- Multiple conflicting animations.

The movement should be slow enough to be visually enjoyable but not so slow that it delays the shopping experience.

Use a carefully tuned duration appropriate to the layout and device.

## 5.2 Animation architecture

Reuse the existing animation library if the project already has one.

If no suitable solution exists, use an appropriately lightweight implementation.

Possible approaches include:

- Framer Motion.
- Motion.
- CSS transforms.
- RequestAnimationFrame where appropriate.

Do not install unnecessary animation dependencies.

Use a temporary overlay or equivalent mechanism.

Important implementation details:

- Use the real product image.
- Calculate source and destination positions dynamically.
- Handle responsive navbar positions.
- Handle scrolling.
- Handle different product card layouts.
- Handle sticky headers.
- Handle mobile navigation.
- Handle unavailable image sources.
- Clean up temporary DOM elements.
- Prevent memory leaks.
- Avoid unnecessary React re-renders.
- Do not interfere with product-card navigation.
- Respect reduced-motion preferences.

## 5.3 Animation accessibility

Respect:

`prefers-reduced-motion`

If the user has requested reduced motion:

- Disable or simplify the flight animation.
- Preserve immediate cart functionality.
- Show a subtle non-motion confirmation instead.

The cart must work even if animation fails.

**Animation must never control the actual cart business logic.**

The cart operation should succeed independently, and the animation should be a visual enhancement only.

## 5.4 Animation consistency

Support:

- Product card Add to Cart.
- Product Details Add to Cart.
- Responsive layouts.
- Desktop.
- Tablet.
- Mobile.

Use the correct source image and correct cart icon destination.

Avoid animating the wrong image when multiple products are visible.

Handle rapid successive user actions without leaving stale animation elements.

