# PHASE 7 — DATABASE SCALABILITY & LARGE PRODUCT CATALOG SUPPORT

NOVARA must be architected to support substantial future growth, including hundreds of thousands of products and potentially larger datasets.

Do not optimize based on assumptions alone.

Inspect actual query patterns and measure performance.

## 7.1 Database indexing

Audit all high-traffic database queries.

Create appropriate indexes for:

- Product slug.
- Product status.
- Product category.
- Product brand.
- Product creation date.
- Product price where frequently filtered.
- Product search fields.
- Variant relationships.
- Inventory lookups.
- Cart ownership.
- Order ownership.
- Order status.
- Order creation date.
- Payment transaction references.
- User email.
- Provider identities.
- Relevant foreign keys.

Use compound indexes where the query patterns justify them.

Do not blindly index every field.

Too many indexes increase storage and write costs.

Use database query plans and execution statistics where available.

## 7.2 Efficient product queries

Avoid:

- Loading every product at once.
- Unbounded database queries.
- N+1 query patterns.
- Repeated unnecessary population.
- Large unnecessary document projections.
- Loading unused images and fields.
- Fetching all product variants when not required.

Implement:

- Server-side pagination.
- Efficient filtering.
- Sorting.
- Search.
- Selective field projection.
- Proper query limits.
- Appropriate cursor-based pagination where useful.

Do not use frontend-only filtering for very large product datasets.

## 7.3 Search architecture

Inspect the existing search implementation.

Improve it according to the current database and project requirements.

Consider:

- Indexed search.
- Search normalization.
- Category filters.
- Brand filters.
- Price filters.
- Availability filters.
- Pagination.
- Sorting.
- Debounced frontend requests.

Do not introduce a separate search infrastructure unless measurements and project requirements justify it.

## 7.4 Caching

Implement caching only where it is safe and useful.

Potential candidates:

- Public product catalog data.
- Category lists.
- Brand lists.
- Public configuration.
- Frequently accessed read-only data.

Use appropriate cache invalidation when data changes.

Never serve stale prices, stock availability, payment status, or security-sensitive user data as authoritative.

If Redis or another cache is already present, reuse it appropriately.

Do not add infrastructure dependencies without a clear reason.

## 7.5 Database connection management

Verify:

- Connection pooling.
- Connection limits.
- Query timeouts.
- Retry behavior.
- Graceful shutdown.
- Database unavailable handling.
- Transaction safety.

Prevent connection leaks and uncontrolled connection creation.

## 7.6 Load and performance testing

Use realistic test datasets.

Test product browsing and search with large datasets, including a representative dataset of at least 100,000 products when the environment permits.

Measure:

- Query latency.
- Pagination performance.
- Memory usage.
